    {
      "id": "clm:fc98b943909af3ce4b01d33605c4be8a36e4b07eae9df56d0f383cc490c46374",
      "text": "Two published meta-analyses of randomized trials of coenzyme Q10 in Parkinson disease reported no significant benefit over placebo: a 2016 analysis of five RCTs (981 patients) found that the overall effect did not favor either group for total UPDRS, and a 2017 analysis of eight studies (899 patients) found a pooled WMD of 1.02 for UPDRS part 3 (p = 0.54) and stated that routine CoQ10 treatment of PD could not be recommended. (high confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26553164/",
      "also": [
        "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27830343/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:174f7d6823b60d44e03a9903b9668b154b767ed869cd8b254ee1ab497b9260bb",
        "ev:5e270888fb10dfbf92145ee1158ad86d220b1bafb63883e2602b98bedc0a05a5",
        "ev:814c887869de4a32ddf0fab6d19f5fa52dcfcc60d7ab667505c72b914234bd8e",
        "ev:8cf68e687a7fc8c934dbff3eee2e58c46d9b81f2b703830f25aa0f565aace0cf",
        "ev:91bd477167979237cefa5211ceee151b124233f9d0cfbc349368d9ec694a1d5f"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:9550ef3eaf5d54b39c3bb89e286effeccd32f126fe57ed12515ff9a49f0cb08d",
      "text": "A 2014 Cochrane review (searches to 10 November 2013; two RCTs, 194 patients) reported no clear evidence of an effect of creatine on motor function (MD -0.26; 95% CI -4.39 to 3.88; low quality evidence) and judged the evidence base limited by risk of bias, small sample sizes and short duration, and a 2017 BMC Neurology meta-analysis (five RCTs, 1339 participants) reported no significant differences in total, mental, activities of daily living or motor UPDRS scores, with an improvement in Schwab & England Scale scores, and concluded that creatine has no observed benefit in PD. (high confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10196714/",
      "also": [
        "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5457735/"
      ],
