      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:c56e2d95c9264e4359163e780404fd513ae99a8adf20b9a5e7686fff99f17792",
      "text": "Published conclusions on isradipine in early Parkinson disease differ: the STEADY-PD III trial (336 patients; 5 mg of immediate-release isradipine twice daily or placebo for 36 months) reported that long-term treatment did not slow clinical progression (treatment effect -0.27 point, P = 0.85), whereas a re-analysis of STEADY-PD II data (10 mg/day extended-release isradipine) reported significantly smaller UPDRS scores than placebo at the end of the 52-week trial, with post hoc adjustment for symptomatic therapy diminishing the statistical significance, and suggested that clinically attainable brain exposure to isradipine may slow early-stage progression. (high confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7465126/",
      "also": [
        "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8922308/"
      ],
