    {
      "id": "clm:41787df49a317d47613dac24d52b24e9a2c2f2146d997c4bc173b1d833672f9f",
      "text": "NICE guideline NG71 (published 19 July 2017), section 1.6 on pharmacological neuroprotective therapy, recommends against using vitamin E as a neuroprotective therapy for people with Parkinson's disease (1.6.1) and against using co-enzyme Q10 as a neuroprotective therapy except in the context of clinical trials (1.6.2), both marked as 2006, amended 2017; a 2026 review reports that CoQ10 is classified as non-efficacious for delaying progression of PD by both the International Parkinson & Movement Disorders Society and the National Institute for Health Care Excellence at the time of writing. (medium confidence) (verified as of 2026-11-27)",
      "source_url": "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng71/chapter/Recommendations",
      "also": [
        "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13442935/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:1e8fb76b233a0706554a7144153bbe341aa08c0f61aa66097fa17078a231488c",
        "ev:69225d0b64bd3c5f9797300b08b9124ee047085df964cfccc49654767ccde52f",
        "ev:8634cb85d758a90841a7950f6a915c5ef829eac9e3afc63ff16ff4a7e984528c",
        "ev:de58cec478b97b7b17df62c26e055110a61f6faacfbe3b6a8497b1ff6b55b5f2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:6705933d08b7fd07812df7482f6623adf52df3da1735b36e61b07dbfeb26b28d",
      "text": "NICE guideline NG71 (published 19 July 2017) recommends in 1.7.13 that people with Parkinson's disease be advised to take a vitamin D supplement, in 1.7.14 that creatine supplements not be offered to people with Parkinson's disease, and in 1.7.15 that people with Parkinson's disease be advised not to take over-the-counter dietary supplements without first consulting their pharmacist or other healthcare professional; a 2026 review distinguishes supplements used to correct deficiencies, such as vitamin D for bone health, which it places outside its scope, from supplements explored for disease-modifying potential. (medium confidence) (verified as of 2026-11-27)",
      "source_url": "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng71/chapter/Recommendations",
      "also": [
        "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13442935/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:69225d0b64bd3c5f9797300b08b9124ee047085df964cfccc49654767ccde52f",
        "ev:6fdb78eee3109cd730aecd5acccade3dfd35fb76ab586b03bb6ca4b9c3610203",
        "ev:a00e48c531272dbb74cf7457972a4860fb7f6662d0548157d1c9496e1722e0bc",
        "ev:db479d450c0192f52bd5c84483e55a24fcce0a460275aa377c65091bcbd6dcb1",
        "ev:de58cec478b97b7b17df62c26e055110a61f6faacfbe3b6a8497b1ff6b55b5f2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:a1c27124fab424807efc9d26ce7112a62feb8d05fd144d3beac0834fa554f356",
      "text": "The American Academy of Neurology practice parameter on neuroprotective strategies and alternative therapies for Parkinson disease (Neurology 2006) reported the conclusions that no treatment has been shown to be neuroprotective and that there is no evidence that vitamin or food additives can improve motor function in PD; a 2026 review states that multiple phase II and III trials of pharmacological agents aimed at modulating PD pathophysiological hallmarks all failed to demonstrate significant disease-modifying effects. (medium confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16606908/",
      "also": [
        "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13442935/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:4607d7c0aa9f16c9fa147fb7ed313f7db30c30af9956f8af8f20af72ebb68561",
        "ev:4de66617b09179043f2e580fe433c608561436ea2c11b1fbd55eb38a76b1cdee",
        "ev:69225d0b64bd3c5f9797300b08b9124ee047085df964cfccc49654767ccde52f",
        "ev:c17885107b39992d578e923bde704b134ea1141f8094f66479899b0e0a788606",
        "ev:c61dde40c0dd0201e0e61ee4610decb600cc24dcc58c90f9f984b0e370e78d94"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:f330c57521eefdd769046d6b89f64ddee62aecd1cda41fe3fce4d3c7c2006e62",
      "text": "In the SURE-PD3 randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 3 trial (298 participants with early PD at 58 US sites; inosine titrated to raise serum urate to 7.1-8.0 mg/dL, n = 149, versus placebo, n = 149, for up to 2 years), the study closed early after a prespecified interim futility analysis and MDS-UPDRS progression rates did not differ significantly (11.1 points per year for inosine versus 9.9 for placebo; difference 1.26, 95% CI -0.59 to 3.11; P = .18); more kidney stones occurred with inosine (7.0 versus 1.4 stones per 100 patient-years) and the authors concluded the findings do not support inosine as a treatment for early PD. (high confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8441591/",
      "also": [
        "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13442935/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
