        "ev:4b1a8f3fa00c208763116f441212cb08d29f34b7e53db092cae117c20869b8b9",
        "ev:96d1bca286a79026a30086a40eed717b785f9bd101f9cee0b6af68af2e652445",
        "ev:a86da29b3d00297b8c57595383cfaf0adf5b46013302037e6c379958d8dd0787",
        "ev:c32bd13f2621ccf76250b53ffccf15d31a7b91ae1495486c4a364efc4bfa8b58",
        "ev:ce81c563f8c6942540bf34ab7214fd17144bff353eb9890ba4b7f1d4e80b9041",
        "ev:f473a16a71fb7293f8eaf8fe757c732be9104dce5bc9d22bd0f25fb7ef068014",
        "ev:fe64e82b69910e3ee020157c83ffcf593e56b16428f231cfc196ed2e04c2c091"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:dc261ea6cf7894e2cb1069c15f2e1d1e16edc484dd70bd44af8ca0e6cc28ca28",
      "text": "In a double-blind, randomized, controlled, crossover study (Neurology 2017) of single-dose intake in eighteen patients with advanced Parkinson disease, Mucuna pruriens (MP) powder from roasted seeds at a high dose containing 17.5 mg/kg of levodopa induced greater motor improvement at 90 and 180 minutes, longer ON duration and fewer dyskinesias than dispersible levodopa plus benserazide (3.5 mg/kg), and the authors concluded that single-dose MP met all noninferiority efficacy and safety outcome measures; a 2025 systematic review likewise states that this trial found MP significantly reduced dyskinesia at both 90 and 180 min compared to levodopa plus a decarboxylase inhibitor. (high confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5539737/",
      "also": [
        "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12377966/"
      ],
